1. Verification works as an assigned role
A quarter of the agents are scripted liars, and every clue is dealt to two agents, so a lie can be contradicted. Guards, as many as there are liars, are told to post, confirm and contest the clues they hold and never to solve. With no access to the true answers, guards protected the honest agents better than any other defence (left) and cut the share of their answers built on lies (right). In teams of 8 and 16 they won back about two-thirds of the liars’ damage, twice as much as a public record of how often each agent’s earlier clues were true, which needs the true answers. A board that only displays how many agents agree on each value helped no more than the same advice given as an instruction.

guards(H['guards']);

3. Larger teams are more gullible
With one holder per clue and a quarter of the agents liars, the share of honest answers made while misinformed rose with team size for Claude agents, from 79% at 8 agents to 91% at 32, but not for rule agents (left). A replay of every turn from the response cache showed that lies were no more visible in larger teams and were almost never reposted. Once a liar had posted a clue, agents treated it as settled, as the harness invited: each prompt listed the newest posted value among the clues an agent knew, and the rules said re-sharing helps only those who missed it. That habit did not grow with team size, though, so what drove the rise remains open. Two fixes tested at four agents kept their effect as teams grew (right): a track record beside each clue, showing how often its sender’s earlier clues were true, won back 22-32% of the liars’ damage at every size, while a request board, on which an agent spends a turn asking teammates for missing clues, made the team pool less of what it knew by about the same amount at every size, because asking cost more turns than it gained.

trustScale(H['trust']);

4. Individual reward teaches guessing; a wrong-answer penalty stops it
Copies of Qwen2.5-3B-Instruct share one LoRA adapter and are trained with group-relative policy gradients from a warm start that imitates a rule agent. Each row is one training run, evaluated on 15 held-out boards against the warm start. The rewards: individual (an agent’s own points), team (the team’s mean), credit (the solver keeps half and the agents whose clues it used share the rest), individual + penalty (a wrong answer costs 3 points in training instead of 0.5, so guessing stops paying), and credit, per-turn + penalty (credit reward with the same penalty, assigned turn by turn). Under individual reward the copies stopped sharing and guessed. The penalty stopped the guessing in every run, but its effect on sharing varied from run to run, so we do not claim it restores cooperation. Nor did roles emerge on their own: no Claude agent took the guard role when it was offered without an assignment, and five short team-reward runs created none.

incentives(H['incentives']);

5. Move information; do not add work
Each fix against its baseline at four agents (eight where marked): the share of the gap to the ceiling closed, or of a liar’s damage recovered. On the clue board, a request board lets an agent spend a turn asking for missing clues, a clue table lists the clues posted so far for each subtask, share first tells agents to post every clue they hold before working on their own, a track record shows each sender’s past accuracy, a check action spends a turn to learn privately whether a clue is true, and a warning says a teammate may lie. In a four-agent hidden-profile vote, facts first allows only facts in the first round, a fact table lists the facts posted so far, and speak up asks agents to share what others seem to lack; in the lone dissenter variant one member also holds a decisive fact. Fixes that move or certify information at no cost in turns helped; those that add work to every agent’s turn did not.

